\documentclass{article}
\title{Structured Pruning of Convolutional Networks at Initialisation}
\author{S. Meissner and H. Traore}
\date{January 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Pruning at initialisation removes entire filters before training begins.
We denote the keep ratio by \tq and sweep it across two orders of magnitude
to understand when accuracy collapses.

\section{Criterion}
Each filter is scored by the sensitivity of the training loss to its removal.
The validation metric val_acc$ is recomputed after every pruning round,
and filters are removed in ascending order of their scores.

\section{Findings}
Our main empirical findings are:
\begin{itemize}
\item sensitivity scores transfer across datasets of similar scale;
\item accuracy is preserved down to very low keep ratios on wide networks;
\item narrow networks collapse abruptly below a critical ratio.
\section{Discussion}
The abrupt collapse of narrow networks suggests that width,
rather than depth, determines the pruning budget available at initialisation.
\end{document}

